 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Memory Complexity:** The fine-tuning process significantly reduces the number of Gaussians compared to the initial feed-forward generation, thereby reducing memory consumption [S1].
-- **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods, leveraging powerful pretrained weights [S1].
-- **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Strengths:** Accelerates network training and enables faster convergence compared to other test-time optimization methods [S1]. Reduces memory consumption by pruning redundant primitives [S1].
-- **Limitations:** The initial feed-forward generation may produce redundant Gaussians, requiring effective pruning strategies [S1].
-- **When to use:** When starting from a dense, pre-generated set of Gaussians (e.g., from a feed-forward model) and aiming for high-fidelity reconstruction with reduced memory footprint.
-- **When not:** When starting from sparse SfM points and requiring initial densification from scratch.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Accelerated convergence compared to test-time optimization methods due to pretrained priors [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced memory consumption compared to the initial feed-forward state, as redundant Gaussians are pruned [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence, high fidelity, reduced memory footprint through pruning.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward prediction; requires careful tuning of densification/pruning thresholds to avoid artifacts.
+*   **When to use:** When high-fidelity synthesis is required and computational resources for initial dense initialization are limited or when leveraging pretrained models is beneficial.
+*   **When not to use:** When the initial feed-forward prediction is poor or when real-time inference speed is the primary constraint and pruning overhead is unacceptable.
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, as described in EVolSplat, was introduced to leverage pretrained priors for efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting for urban view synthesis [S1]. The method was developed to address the inefficiencies of standard test-time optimization by using a direct feed-forward process for initialization, followed by a fine-tuning stage that optimizes all geometry and color attributes while enabling growing and pruning [S1].
+Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the EVolSplat paper, which proposes an efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting method for urban view synthesis [S1]. The method leverages pretrained weights to accelerate network training and enable faster convergence compared to other test-time optimization methods [S1]. It addresses the issue of redundant initial Gaussians by enabling growing and pruning during the fine-tuning phase [S1].
 
